                                        <div class="person-dialog__header margin-trim">
                                            <h2 class="type-h3">Sally George</h2>

                                                                                            <p class="type-h5">Aline Solness</p>
                                                                                    </div>

                                        <div class="person-dialog__bio margin-trim type-body-l">
                                            <p>Sally trained at Guildhall School of Music and Drama. MA Royal Central School of Speech and Drama. She has worked extensively as an actress for over four decades, starting her career with several years as a member of The Royal Shakespeare Company.</p>
<p>Theatre includes: RSC: <em>The Taming of the Shrew</em>, <em>Oedipus</em>; <em>The New Inn</em>,<em> Richard III</em>; (Australia); <em>Penny for a Song</em>; <em>The Merry Wives of Windsor</em>; <em>Temptation</em>, <em>Misalliance</em>,<em> Voices from Prison</em>; <em>Fenn</em>, <em>Titus Andronicus</em>;<em> The Taming of the Shrew</em> (Regional tour and Japan). <em>People Places and Things</em>: National Theatre and West End. <em>When We Are Married</em> (National Tour), <em>Oedipus</em>: Almeida Theatre, <em>Case of The Poet</em>, <em>My Matisse</em>, <em>You Can’t Take It With You</em>, <em>Ghosts</em>, <em>Women Laughing</em>: Kings Head Theatre. <em>My Matisse</em> (one woman show) New End Theatre and Edinburgh festival. <em>The Comedy of Errors</em> and<em> The Jolly Potters</em>: New Vic Theatre Stoke. <em>Larkin With Women</em>: West Yorkshire Playhouse. <em>Something Blue</em>: Stephen Joseph Theatre Scarborough; <em>House and Garden</em>: Theatre Royal Northampton, <em>Traveller Without Luggage</em>: Critics Up For Review Battersea Arts. <em>Richard III</em>: Northern Broadsides Riverside Studios, Tower of London and Copenhagen; <em>Storm in A Flower Vase</em>, West End, <em>Lady Mosley’s Suite</em>: Trafalgar Studios. <em>An Elizabethan Christmas</em>: Hampton Court Palace. <em>Kitchen Sink</em>: Hornchurch, <em>Positive</em>, <em>The Rise and Fall of Little Voice</em>: Park Theatre and Bolton Octagon.</p>
<p>TV includes: <em>Holby City</em>, <em>Casualty</em>,<em> Footballers Wives</em>, <em>Hollyoaks</em>, <em>Doctors</em>,<em> Daziel and Pascoe</em>, <em>Heartbeat</em>, <em>Out of Sight</em>, <em>The Bill</em>, <em>EastEnders</em>, <em>Persuasion</em>,<em> Measure for Measure</em>, <em>Buddha of Suburbia</em>.</p>
<p>Film: <em>Women at War</em>, <em>The Diaries</em>, <em>Love Somehow</em> (three best actress awards), <em>The Light Retracts the Shadows</em>, <em>Absolution</em>, <em>The Guard of Auschwitz</em>, <em>The Diary of Winifred Meeks</em>,<em> About a Girl</em>. (Two best supporting actress awards). <em>Tender Art</em>, <em>Noble Genius</em>. BBC Radio; <em>The Roy Orbison Story</em>, <em>Temptation</em>, <em>Richard III</em>, <em>King Street Juniors Revisited</em>. Her new play <em>I Can Die Too</em> co -written with Alan Cumming and Frances Ruffelle premiered earlier this year at The Pitlochry Festival Theatre. She is soon to tour the US in a new play: <em>Love Notes</em> playing Clara Schumann. And is thrilled to be here at the Arcola in this Ibsen classic.</p>

                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </article>
                            </template>
                                            </li>
                                                                                                                                        
                    <li class="person not-prose has-image has-dark-grey-background-color">
